**Chapter 6: Pub Philosophy**

The Eagle on Bene’t Street smelled of three centuries of spilled ale, damp wool, and the thick, acrid haze of cigarette smoke. It was a smell that James Adamson had come to associate with the confusing borderland between genius and insanity.

Thirty years prior, in this very room, Francis Crick had burst through the doors to announce that he and Watson had discovered the secret of life. Tonight, James was merely trying to discover the bottom of a pint of Abbot Ale before his brain short-circuited.

"You look like you're vibrating," Eleanor said. She was nursing a gin and tonic, watching him with that analytical gaze usually reserved for her primitive AI scripts. "If you shake the table again, my drink goes in your lap."

James gripped his pint glass with both hands to steady himself. "I can't turn it off, El. The VAX... the pattern. It’s still running behind my eyes."

"That’s called eye strain, James. It comes from staring at green phosphorous for twelve hours straight."

"No," James insisted, taking a gulp of the lukewarm, copper-tasting beer. "It’s the efficiency of it. That’s what’s haunting me."

The pub was roaring with Friday night energy. Underneath the ceiling, which was still scorched with the candle-smoke signatures of WWII airmen, students and dons debated everything from Thatcher’s economic policy to the merits of The Human League. But James felt detached, trapped in a conceptual bubble.

"Explain," Eleanor commanded, leaning forward.

James set his glass down on a soggy cardboard coaster. "Think about the arcade. Think about *Space Invaders*."

Eleanor rolled her eyes. "Oh god. Are we back to video games?"

"Just listen. In *Space Invaders*, the computer doesn't render fifty unique aliens. It doesn't have the memory for that. It renders one sprite—one collection of pixels—and tells the screen to draw it fifty times at fifty different coordinates. It’s a loop. A memory-saving trick."

"Right. That’s basic programming. Pointer logic."

"Exactly," James said, his voice dropping to a conspiratorial whisper. "So, I’m looking at this data from the radio telescope. I’m seeing this glitch. This distinctive, jagged little energetic hiccup. And I see it in a star cluster forty light-years away, and then I see the *exact same glitch* in background radiation from a completely different sector."

"Coincidence. Or a sensor artifact. You said yourself the hardware is older than we are."

"It’s not a coincidence," James said, tapping his temple. "It’s a reused asset. The universe is saving memory."

Eleanor laughed, a bright, sharp sound that cut through the murmur of the pub. "Brilliant. You think reality is running out of RAM? Did God forget to upgrade the motherboard?"

"Mock all you want," James grinned, the alcohol finally beginning to soften the jagged edges of his anxiety. "But Occam’s Razor, right? What’s more likely: that there are ten octillion distinct, unique electrons in the observable universe, all magically possessing the exact same mass to the nineteenth decimal point, the exact same charge, the exact same spin... or that there’s just one?"

Eleanor stopped laughing. She paused, her glass halfway to her mouth. She lowered it slowly.

"You’re not talking about video games anymore," she said. "You’re talking about Wheeler."

James blinked. "Who?"

"John Wheeler. Princeton." Eleanor swirled her ice cubes. "He called Richard Feynman in the forties. Middle of the night, apparently. He said, 'Feynman, I know why all electrons have the same charge and the same mass.' And Feynman asked why. And Wheeler said, 'Because they are all the same electron.'"

James felt a strange, cold prickle run down his neck, totally unrelated to the drafty door. He knew the name Feynman, of course—every physics student worshipped the ground the man walked on—but he hadn’t heard this specific anecdote. "Go on."

"It’s the One-Electron Universe postulate," Eleanor recited, her voice slipping into lecture mode. "Imagine space-time as a giant loom. A single particle enters from the Big Bang. It travels to the end of time, turns around, travels back to the beginning, turns around again. Back and forth. Weaving the fabric of reality. When it goes forward in time, we see an electron. When it travels backward..."

"...we see a positron," James finished, the physics clicking into place instantly. "Antimatter. It has the same mass, opposite charge. Mathematically, a positron is indistinguishable from an electron moving backward in time."

"Precisely," Eleanor said. "Wheeler dropped the idea eventually. Too many holes in it. For one, there should be an equal amount of matter and antimatter if it’s one thread looping back and forth. We don't see that. The universe is mostly matter."

"Maybe the thread is hiding," James murmured, staring into the amber depths of his ale. He grabbed a handful of peanuts from the bowl in the center of the table and began arranging them on the dark wood. "Forward... backward... forward."

"James, don't play with your food."

"Look at the cross-section, El." He moved his hand in a slicing motion over the peanuts. "If I take a slice of time—'Now'—and I cut through this weave, I see billions of peanuts. Billions of particles. They look separate. They look like a crowd. But they aren't."

He looked up, his eyes wide and slightly bloodshot.

"It’s just one lonely traveler," he whispered. "Buzzing around so fast it looks like a population."

Eleanor sighed and ate one of the particles. "It’s a fun thought experiment, James. Great for pub philosophy. But it’s bad science. If there was only one electron, the interaction delays would be infinite. The causal loops would crash the system."

"Unless..." James trailed off. The VAX. The crash. The glitch.

"Unless what?"

"Unless the system *is* crashing," James said, a manic grin spreading across his face. "Unless that glitch I found is the seam. The moment where the particle turns around. The refresh rate of the universe."

Eleanor shook her head, smiling affectionately. "You are completely mad. You know that? You’ve been in Cambridge two terms and you’re already rewriting quantum mechanics based on a game of *Galaga*."

"Maybe," James laughed, finally picking up his drink again. He clinked his glass against hers. "To the Lonely Electron. Long may he wave."

"To the Lonely Electron," Eleanor agreed. "And to you getting some sleep."

They finished their drinks and stumbled out into the cool Cambridge night. The air was sharp and smelled of damp stone and bicycle rust. As they walked back toward the Trinity gatehouse, Eleanor chattered about her AI project—something about heuristic search trees—but James had stopped listening.

He looked up. The sky was clear, a rare treat for an English November. The stars were hard, diamond-like points of light hanging in the void.

*Wheeler dropped it,* Eleanor had said. *Too many holes.*

But Wheeler didn't have a VAX-11/780. Wheeler didn't have the data James had seen.

James swayed slightly, the alcohol buzzing in his blood, but his mind was sobering up with terrifying speed. He looked at a streetlamp. He imagined the swarm of electrons powering the bulb. He looked at his own hand, the skin pale under the sodium light.

*Forward in time. Backward in time.*

If it was true—if it was even remotely true—it meant that every electron in his body was the same electron that was in Eleanor’s body. The same electron that was in the star overhead. The same electron that was in the pavement.

It meant that separation was an illusion. It meant that time was just a way to keep everything from happening at once.

"James?" Eleanor called out from a few paces ahead. "You coming? Or are you calculating the trajectory of the pavement?"

"Coming," James called back.

He jogged to catch up, laughing off the shiver in his spine. It was just a joke. Just pub philosophy. The sort of thing you say to impress a girl or sound clever after two pints.

But as he walked through the Great Gate of Trinity, passing under the statue of Henry VIII holding a chair leg instead of a sceptre, James Adamson knew he wouldn't sleep tonight.

His subconscious had clamped onto the idea like a terrier with a rat. The glitch in the data wasn't noise. It was a footprint. And if he followed the footprint, he might just find the thing that made it.

He needed to get back to the VAX. He needed to trace the thread. Not to see where it went, but to see when it turned around.

"Hey, El," he said as they reached the fork in the path where the dorms split from the labs.

"Yeah?"

"I think I left my scarf in the computer lab."

Eleanor gave him a look that suggested she knew exactly what he was doing, and that it had nothing to do with knitwear. "Don't break the mainframe, James. Halloway will have your hide."

"I won't break it," James said, turning toward the dim glow of the computer building. "I'm just going to ask it a question."

He walked away into the shadows, his mind already racing ahead, weaving back and forth, stitching the impossible into the inevitable.







